---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Operating Model"
  description="How SCSDA is organised: research groups join with their own teams under a shared framework, projects sign through the academy, and a small in-house team runs delivery, finance and commercialisation."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating Model</p>
        <h2>A framework research groups choose to join.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        SCSDA was not built by hiring a large staff. It was built as an
        arrangement that research groups had a reason to join, and the
        arrangement did the work. This page sets out how the academy is
        organised, because the model itself is part of what the academy
        offers to prospective partners.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section surface">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How It Works</p>
        <h2>Five elements of the model.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each element is designed so that participation is worthwhile for the
        joining group on its own terms, rather than depending on reporting
        lines or institutional obligation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell output-grid">
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>1. Groups join with their own teams</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Academics join together with the researchers they already lead.</li>
          <li>They plug into a shared framework: research directions, task
            objectives, funding and indicators, and access to equipment,
            instruments and space.</li>
          <li>No one transfers employment to the academy.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>2. Projects sign through the academy</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>External projects won by participating groups are contracted in
            the academy's name.</li>
          <li>This gives the platform a coherent delivery record rather than a
            scatter of individual engagements.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>3. A small in-house team runs operations</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>The academy directly employs a core team covering finance,
            technical delivery, management, market development and
            commercialisation.</li>
          <li>Research groups are freed from administration; the platform keeps
            delivery discipline in one place.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>4. Mature results can spin out</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Where results reach commercial maturity, staff can establish a
            company around them.</li>
          <li>The academy takes an equity holding and continues to support the
            venture rather than stepping away from it.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>5. Overseas centres run the same model</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Joint centres established with partner institutions abroad follow
            the same framework.</li>
          <li>Cooperation funding flows in both directions: the academy pays the
            partner side for its participation.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell article narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why This Matters</p>
      <h2>The arrangement is the capability.</h2>
      <p>
        A research platform that depends on direct authority can only grow as
        fast as it can hire. A platform built as a framework grows whenever a
        capable group finds it worth joining. During its construction period
        the academy developed twelve joint bilateral research centres and
        laboratories, four of them overseas, and cumulatively recruited
        forty-five researchers at master's level and above, alongside
        forty-six postgraduate and doctoral participants and seventeen
        flexibly engaged senior experts.
      </p>
      <p>
        These figures come from the government-accepted construction-period
        report covering 2019 to 2023. They are presented here to show how the
        model performed, not as a claim about current staffing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section surface">
    <div class="shell article narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working With Us</p>
      <h2>Who this model suits.</h2>
      <p>
        The framework works best for research groups that already have their
        own people and direction, and that want delivery infrastructure around
        their work: contracting, finance, industry access and a route to
        commercialisation. It also suits partner institutions abroad that want
        a working relationship in China with defined funding flows rather than
        a memorandum of understanding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Individual cooperation agreements are held with academics at a number
        of universities. The academy does not present these as
        institution-level partnerships, and does not list partner names
        publicly, since an agreement with a researcher is not an endorsement
        by their employer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
